  "notes": "An Uptown-then-Sony staff producer, and the research calls his the \"interpolation-and-loop school that made hip-hop soul a format\". Its era is given as 1989–2005; **the window is trimmed to 1990–2005 here for Gate 2**, which excludes none of the credits the entry names. The band is \"79–95 straight (backbeat 2&4)\", mode 95, the sourced tempo of the C-major record. Three drum deltas from the founding template: \"a hard boom-bap kick and snare laid over a looped source — backbeat flat on 2&4 with an added clap, 16th hats thin, and **the source record's drums left audible**\". So the `velocityTiers` accent sits at 110–126 over a 72–88 main (the hard kit over the soft loop), the clap is offset 3–9 ms and the snare 4–10 ms with `quantizeStrength` at 0.6 — the loop's own timing showing through — and the hat lane is a thin continuous 16th at 0.18. Harmony is \"static 2- or 4-bar harmony from the loop, with the topline written to fit the sample's mode rather than the reverse\", and the keys are the entry's own: **C major and A major, because the flipped 70s/80s sources are major**, which is the identifying oddity in a minor-leaning lane. So the diatonic major families lead, `harmonicRhythm` is weighted to `vamp`, and the melody's chord-tone bias is high because it is written *to* the loop. \"He plays keyboards and sings backgrounds on his own productions\" is the counter at +12. The low end is \"the looped source bassline carries the part; sub-kick doubles it\", so no `bass808` block and a bass that follows roots at 0.82. ⚠ Confidence is **low**: the research rates it medium-low and says the drum detail is board-level. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `C`, `A`, and the remaining values `F`, `G`, `Am`, together with all 5 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry.",
